  {
    "sha": "b3a5f38f51cae10a9f6af3ba1c31f9f3c8685844",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoU8JdoAKGIzYTVmMzhmNTFjYWUxMGE5ZjZhZjNiYTFjMzFmOWYzYzg2ODU4NDQ",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-13T04:23:47Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-13T04:23:47Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(sim): wire laws judge each skim pass at its own kerf (#538 over #534)\n\nMain's wire verify measured every move against the rough condition's\nkerf (0.125 + 0.04 = 0.165 mm), so skim 1's path at its own offset\n0.145 (0.125 + 0.02) read as a 0.0200 mm gouge at move 8 although its\nreal kerf edge lies on the profile. WireJob now carries each skim's\nown kerf offset; the path is split at each return to the threading\npoint (the rule the post uses) and each move is judged at its own\npass's half-width - never narrower than that pass's real kerf. The\nthreading law takes the widest pass; the slug law sweeps each pass at\nits own offset. A path that does not return once per pass, or an open\nrun with skims, is refused by name (also at the door in check_passes).\n\nRefs #538\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
